                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "For me this is both a step down from the previous game and doesn't make good use of VR.\n\nThere's no campaign, just lots of individual preset missions you can choose from. There are a LOT of missions (100 I think) but the previous game had a campaign to link the fights together and made you more attached to your ships as they had to survive between fights.\n\nThe controls aren't clear - maybe there was a tutorial I missed but it looks like to give movement orders you click or hold (it doesn't seem consistent EDIT2: was a bug which occurred when using a Rift, now fixed) but all ships must target the same enemy per turn, at least that's what it looks like when you click an enemy. I can't even find a way to leave a mission except by winning or losing it. There doesn't appear to be any options menu or in-combat menu in the game. EDIT2: There's also no manual to explain how to play or what each ship type does.\n\nThe use of VR controllers makes it easier to position and orient your ships which is good.\n\nYou don't appear to be able to scale the game world and it's too 'zoomed in' for my liking. I'd like to be able to see and reach more of the map without panning around a lot (squeeze a grip button to pan/drag), and ideally the player should be able to scale up and down to suit the situation. Some missions start with your viewpoint clipping inside an obstacle in the play area. \n\nThere's a severe vignette when panning which was just unhelpful as it prevents you seeing the play area - I couldn't find a way to turn it off. EDIT2: the dev has added a command line option to turn the vignette off.\n\nThe game needlessly requires a 360 degree setup in the mission select area, making it harder for Rift users with front facing setups. You pick up a 'level' cartridge and place it in a slot behind you which could just have easily been in front of you.\n\nI'm disappointed as the first Flotilla was an unusual but interesting and unique game. This unfortunately feels like a stripped down, poorly implemented VR port of that.\n\nEDIT1: I first played on the Rift - having tried the game on the Vive it looks like it doesn't work properly on the Rift. The controls work consistently on the Vive (click to select ship, click to move, click to orient) and special actions for the ships appear, which I didn't see the first time I played. The special actions for the ships are things like flank speed, rapid fire, etc.",
